## V. What problems does it solve in practice

- A data science team at an insurance company needs to test a 
new risk subscription model without waiting months for approval 
access to real customer data.
- A hospital wants to share a research dataset with a 
partner university without running the risk of re-identifying patients.
- A fintech needs to generate realistic test data for its 
staging environment, without replicating production (and without violating your 
own privacy-by-design commitment).
- A regulator wants to understand, in current Portuguese, whether a dataset 
synthetic material presented to you is safe enough to be 
used as proof of compliance — don't want to read a table of 
p-values without context.


In all these cases, what existing tools lack is not
generating similar data — it's the **documented and verifiable** proof that this data is useful and safe for its intended use.

## VI. How it compares to what already exists

There's no shame in comparing: the synthetic data market has mature and well-built tools. The difference with S.Y.N.A.P. is not a "better generative model" — it's a different architectural philosophy.

| | **Typical market approach** | **S.Y.N.A.P.** |

|---|---|---|

| **Generating engine** | A single model (GAN, VAE, or a tabular autoregressive transformer) trained to minimize a global statistical distance | Eight specialized modules, with a statistical + symbolic "dual brain" validating each row in real time |

| **Causal structure** | Generally absent or implicit in the model; Few tools expose or validate an explicit DAG | Explicit DAG, adjusted by d-separation tests, with automatic detection of missing edges |
| **Business Rules** | Typically post-processed (filter after generating) or absent | Logical validation (ASP) integrated into the generation cycle itself, with induction of new rules from approved data |
| **Privacy** | Often a statistical quality report presented as "privacy"; not always tested against real data | DCR, NNDR, exact match and Membership Inference Attack, with **veto** power over statistical fidelity |
| **Utility** | A performance metric, usually on a single target task | TSTR/TRTR across multiple tasks, with aggregate utility retention |
| **Diversity / Model Collapse** | Known risk of GANs/VAEs; rarely explicitly monitored after training | Dedicated module (Homeostasis) that continuously measures entropy and divergence and corrects in real time |

**Transparency** | Proprietary engine, "black box" on most commercial platforms | Open and documented architecture, module by module, with public tests |

**Explainable Blocking** | Usually absent — the tool generates and delivers | Automatic blocking with readable diagnostics, when fidelity or privacy are not sufficient |


Tools like **MOSTLY AI** (Vienna, founded in 2017, now also
associated with Synthea) and **Gretel** (acquired by Nvidia in 2025) are mature commercial platforms with good differential privacy capabilities and PII detection — but they operate as a closed service, revolving around a single generative engine. The **Synthetic Data Vault (SDV)** and the **CTGAN** library, on the open-source side, are excellent academic starting points and have wide adoption — but, as the ecosystem itself recognizes, they do not include privacy or causal/logical validation built-in by default; that is the responsibility of the user. Rule-based simulators (like Synthea, in the healthcare domain) solve a narrower problem well — generating plausible records according to hand-coded clinical logic — but they do not learn the statistics of a real dataset nor produce an auditable privacy verdict. S.Y.N.A.P. doesn't necessarily replace these tools in all scenarios—but it occupies a space that none of them fully cover: generating, validating causally and logically, and **proving** (not just generating) utility and privacy, in a single end-to-end readable system.

## VII. Where Honesty Matters More Than Enthusiasm

This document would not be true to the very spirit of the project if it didn't say where it is still limited:

- Causal learning (PC-algorithm) is simplified—it doesn't fully implement Meek's rules.

- The rule induction engine (ILP) is restricted to a univariate template; it is not a generic ILP.

- No system—this one included—can claim privacy without testing it against real data; when no real holdout is available, S.Y.N.A.P. refuses to pretend it has evaluated, and says so explicitly.

## VIII. Where is it going?

The condition for being "truly disruptive" wasn't rhetoric: it was an
acceptance criterion. A system that proves statistical utility,
privacy security, and practical value—all three, not just one—in
regulated sectors, ceases to be "just another synthetic data tool" and becomes a trusted infrastructure. That's where
S.Y.N.A.P. was built to point, one module at a time.

## IX. An open product, not a closed proof of concept

The condition for being "truly disruptive"—proving utility, privacy, and
practical value—is only credible if it can be scrutinized by anyone,
